## 1. Memory Management & Streaming Architecture

### The Out-Of-Memory (OOM) Pitfall
A naive implementation in Node.js fetching all documents at once via `db.collection.find().toArray()` loads the entire dataset into V8 heap memory. For collections exceeding $50,000$ documents or containing large subdocuments, Node.js exceeds its default memory ceiling ($\approx 1.4\text{ GB}$) and crashes with:
```
FATAL ERROR: Ineffective mark-compacts near heap limit Allocation failed - JavaScript heap out of memory
```

### The Solution: Cursor Streaming with Backpressure
```
┌─────────────────────────┐
│     MongoDB Server      │
└────────────┬────────────┘
             │  Wire Protocol Stream (batchSize: 500)
             ▼
┌─────────────────────────┐
│ MongoDB Cursor Stream   │ (Constant Memory: ~15 MB)
└────────────┬────────────┘
             │  Async Generator / Transform Pipeline
             ▼
┌─────────────────────────┐
│   Transformation &      │ - Type casting
│     Sanitizer           │ - Flattening / Normalization
└────────────┬────────────┘
             │  Batch Buffer (Chunks of 500 rows)
             ▼
┌─────────────────────────┐
│ PostgreSQL Ingestion    │ Multi-Row Parameterized Batch INSERT / COPY
└─────────────────────────┘
```

* **Node.js Implementation Pattern:**
  ```javascript
  const cursor = sourceCollection.find({}).batchSize(500);
  let batch = [];
  for await (const doc of cursor) {
    const transformedRow = transformDocument(doc, mapping);
    batch.push(transformedRow);
    
    if (batch.length >= 500) {
      await insertBatch(pgClient, targetTable, batch);
      batch = [];
      // Trigger GC friendly buffer release
    }
  }
  if (batch.length > 0) {
    await insertBatch(pgClient, targetTable, batch);
  }
  ```

